#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

usage() {
    cat <<'HELP'
Usage: archive-integrity COMMAND ...
       archive-integrity --help

Seal items into an archive, check archives and read what they hold,
in the ClankOS container.
"clankos-run help archive-integrity" prints the protocol.

To seal: plan, save the plan as printed, read it, and apply it by its
SHA-256.

    archive-integrity seal notes/findings.org archives/findings.org > _seal/plan.json
    archive-integrity apply _seal/plan.json HASH
    archive-integrity check . && archive-integrity checkpoint .

HASH is what sha256sum, or shasum -a 256, prints for the plan file.

To retire a document that others link to or that holds open tasks:
salvage, plan the seal, plan the relink from it, then apply the seal
and the relink.

  seal SOURCE DESTINATION [--apply]
      print the plan to seal SOURCE at DESTINATION, in an archive
  write-new DESTINATION [--apply]
      print the plan to seal a new record, read from standard input
  salvage ROOT SOURCE [--dry-run]
      copy each open task of SOURCE, a file or directory about to be
      sealed, to intray.org at ROOT, and close the original where it
      stands, each citing the other; run before the seal is planned.
      --dry-run lists the tasks and writes nothing
  links-into ROOT PATH
      print each link in the Org files under ROOT that leads to PATH
      or into it, by file or by ID, as FILE:LINE: LINK -> FILE
  relink ROOT PLAN
      print the plan to rewrite the links to the item PLAN seals, a
      saved seal plan, in the Org files under ROOT. Made before PLAN is
      applied, and applied after it
  apply PLAN HASH
      apply a reviewed plan, a seal's or a relink's, named by its hash
  check ROOT
      report every archive under ROOT, as JSON
  checkpoint ROOT
      record the ledger heads under ROOT, once the check is clean
  repair ROOT
      restore recorded directories and read bits; remove write bits; never enrols
  convert ROOT
      bring each schema 2 ledger under ROOT to schema 3, once it is clean
  keep ROOT
      move to its keeper each archive under ROOT a keeper is to keep
  recall ROOT
      bring back from its keeper each archive under ROOT a keeper keeps
  link PATH
      print the ipfs:// link to PATH, a sealed path in an archive: the
      citation a document uses for it
  fetch LINK
      print the bytes of the archived file LINK names, an ipfs:// link
      with any path after the CID, from an archive of the working
      directory or of a directory above it: on disk, or from its
      keeper, checked against the CID
  search ROOT QUERY [--mode MODE] [--limit N] [--within CID]
      print each line QUERY is found on in the archives under ROOT, on
      disk or at their keepers, as LINK:LINE:TEXT: grep's shape, with
      the citation fetch takes in place of a path. MODE is literal or
      regex; N caps the hits; CID restricts the search to what is
      beneath one sealed item, file or archive. Exit 1 with no hit
  sign-in URL
      sign in to the keeper at URL and keep the token for later
      commands. Prints an address to open in a browser on this
      machine. Writes pos/tokens.json under $XDG_CONFIG_HOME or
      ~/.config, outside the workspace. Runs with the host's network.

--apply applies a command's own plan at once and prints both.
Paths are within the workspace; bin/clankos-run --help says what the
workspace is and what reaches the container.
Exit: 0 done or clean, 1 findings, 2 refused or usage; 125, 126 and
127 are clankos-run's.
HELP
}

case "${1:-}" in
    ''|help|-h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
esac

run=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/clankos-run
if [ "$1" = sign-in ]; then
    config=${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}
    [ -d "$config/pos" ] || mkdir -p -m 700 "$config/pos"
    exec "$run" --host-network archive-integrity "$@"
fi
exec "$run" archive-integrity "$@"
